    id: 'automorphism',
    title: '4. Automorphism group',
    content: `<p>Aut(X_N) = S_N for the N-point carrier. For N = 4: S4, with |S4| = 24. The automorphism group acts by permuting indices: G_ij &rarr; G_&sigma;(i)&sigma;(j) for &sigma; &isin; S_N.</p>
<p>Gauge groups from the irrep decomposition of S4:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>1 (trivial rep)</strong> &rarr; U(1) &mdash; overall phase symmetry. Eigenvalue &lambda;_U1 = 12.</li>
<li><strong>1' (sign rep)</strong> &rarr; discrete &reals;/2 phase &mdash; interacts with trivial to give the full U(1) charge structure.</li>
<li><strong>2 (doublet)</strong> &rarr; SU(2) &mdash; weak isospin. Eigenvalue &lambda;_SU2 = 4.</li>
<li><strong>3 (standard rep on &complex;&sup4; quotient)</strong> &rarr; SU(3) &mdash; color. Derived from the 3-dimensional subrepresentation orthogonal to trivial.</li>
</ul>
<p>The gauge groups are the irreducible representations of the carrier's symmetry group, cut off at the dimension of the carrier. The SM gauge group is exactly the set of unitary symmetries that preserve the Gram matrix's S4 structure.</p>`
