  {
    id: 'postnikov',
    title: '5. The Postnikov tower &mdash; where the Newton path diverges',
    content: `<p>The carrier programme answers the question: <em>Where does the Newton &rarr; QM &rarr; QFT path diverge from the carrier derivation?</em></p>
<p><strong>The Newton &rarr; QM &rarr; SM path (standard):</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Newton: &reals;&sup3; as configuration space, &reals; as time. Position Q and momentum P as primitive coordinates on phase space.</li>
<li>QM: Promote Q, P to operators on L&sup2;(&reals;&sup3;). Time remains an external parameter. Quantization is a <em>procedure</em> applied to a classical system.</li>
<li>QFT: Promote fields (functions of Q and T) to operators. Gauge groups are added externally by hand.</li>
<li>SM: Postulate SU(3)&times;SU(2)&times;U(1). Write down a Lagrangian with &sim;19 parameters. Fit to data.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>The carrier path:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Carrier: X_N finite set &rarr; Gram G_ij.</li>
<li>Postnikov tower of S4: BS4 has stages with fibers K(&reals;/2, 2), K(&reals;/3, 2), K(&reals;/2, 2) &mdash; these ARE U(1), SU(2), SU(3).</li>
<li>Bottom stage: K(&reals;, 3) &mdash; this is where Q (position), P (momentum), T (time) emerge as <em>derived</em> concepts.</li>
<li>Three projections of G_ij give QM, GR, SM simultaneously, with 4 parameters (Gram eigenvalues 11, 12, 4, 10).</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>The deviation:</strong></p>
<p>The Newton &rarr; QM path treats Q, P, T as the <em>foundation</em> and builds up by quantizing and adding gauge groups. The carrier programme treats the <strong>Postnikov tower of S4</strong> as the foundation and derives Q, P, T as the <strong>bottom rung</strong> of that tower.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Position Q</strong> emerges as the classifying map from the carrier to K(&reals;, 3) &mdash; the lowest stage of the Postnikov tower. The "points" of space are images of this map from the finite carrier set.</li>
<li><strong>Momentum P</strong> is the differential of this classifying map, encoded in the phase differences &theta;_j &minus; &theta;_i of the Gram overlap. The derivative d(&theta;_j &minus; &theta;_i)/dt gives the momentum.</li>
<li><strong>Time T</strong> is the chirality 3-cycle (identity &rarr; left &rarr; right &rarr; identity) in the S4 structure &mdash; a preferred orientation that breaks time-reversal symmetry intrinsically. The three steps of the 3-cycle give three generations of fermions.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>&ldquo;Quantization is the vertical arrow in the Postnikov tower.&rdquo;</strong> The map from higher stages (U(1), SU(2), SU(3) fibers) to the bottom stage (K(&reals;, 3), where Q, P, T live) is what standard physics calls "quantization." The tower <em>is</em> the quantization, and the gauge groups are the higher stages.</p>
<p>The apparent conflict between QM and GR at short distances is a lower-stage phenomenon: at the higher Postnikov stages, the Gram matrix is a single structure whose QM and GR projections are consistent by construction.</p>`
