  {
    id: 'sector',
    title: '6. Three projections from one Gram matrix',
    content: `<p>Same Gram G_ij, three distinct physical theories from three different ways of reading the same matrix:</p>
<table class="operations-table">
  <tr><th>Projection</th><th>Construction</th><th>Physical theory</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>QM</strong></td><td>I = &Sigma; G_ij exp(i(&theta;_j &minus; &theta;_i))</td><td>Quantum interference, Born rule, phase evolution</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>GR</strong></td><td>g_ab(p) = G_ab|_Stab(p)</td><td>Spatial metric from stabilizer, spacetime from fibration</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>SM</strong></td><td>G = V&sup2;&sdot; diag(&lambda;_1,&hellip;,&lambda;_4) &sdot; V</td><td>Gauge groups from S4 irreps, Yukawas from Gram eigenvectors</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Why this single-matrix approach works:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The Gram is <strong>Hermitian</strong> &rarr; eigenvalues are real, probabilities are positive, phase evolution is unitary. This is the QM structure.</li>
